rounds:
  - round: 1
    found: 9
    verified: 4
    detail: >-
      Five finders, nine candidates, adversarial verification confirming four. (1) evict never
      called the new unlink, so the byte arithmetic still existed twice while unlink's comment and
      this WU's own test/v2 note both claimed it was unified - the claim had been written three
      times over. Fixed by extracting Ring::debit as the single place a flow leaves the map and is
      debited, called from both. The fix both finders IMPLIED - routing evict through unlink - was
      refuted by verification as a regression, since it would make every insert O(ring length) and
      drop the last-row floor, so only the two-line arithmetic is shared and each caller keeps its
      own order-mending. (2) unlink's order.retain rebuilt the whole order for a single-id delete,
      a scan plus a hash probe per row under the lock the live capture path contends on; verified
      important because retained_bytes counts only body bytes, so a capture of body-less exchanges
      grows the row count without limit while the byte cap never fires. A single removal now finds
      its one row; a batch still rebuilds, where the cost is proportionate to a scan the filter
      already did. (3) A cursor row can now vanish by deletion as well as eviction, and neither the
      listing contract (scoped to "evicted") nor UC-7 said so, with no test pairing the two - the
      contract sentence now names both causes, UC-7 carries the consequence, and a regression test
      pins it. (4) Two reborrows were dead scaffolding from a borrow error already solved another
      way, each verified by compiling it away. Five refuted: removed.clone() (the returned ids are
      genuinely consumed by tests asserting identity, not just count), the delete()/post() test
      helper duplication (one helper per verb is the file's established convention, as get()/json()
      already show, and a method-parameterised helper would cost call-site clarity), and three
      further candidates. The altitude finder returned a clean verdict resting on the premise that
      unlink was the only place a flow leaves the ring - a premise two other finders had already
      disproved - so its all-clear carried no weight on that question, though its separate layering
      conclusions stood.
  - round: 2
    found: 1
    verified: 1
    detail: >-
      Re-review of the fix as the unreviewed code it was. Two finders independently found the same
      single defect and nothing else: the edit that extracted debit had left the old unlink comment
      sitting above it, so the new function carried two docstrings - the first naming a doomed
      parameter it does not take and asserting the one-helper design the fix had just rejected.
      That is round 1's own finding reintroduced by the edit that fixed it, which is the argument
      for re-reviewing a fix rather than trusting it. Corrected in 5846170, with a sweep confirming
      no other stale claim survives in code or docs. The correctness finder reported no findings on
      the three properties flagged as riskiest in the fix: debit's behaviour in both callers, the
      inverted retain predicate, and duplicate ids in doomed.
